 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1787). Words follow the Schirmer vocal score's spelling, punctuation and syllables: \"un altra\" as two words, un sung with the first syllable of altra (libretto un'altra); \"dond'escono\" as one word (libretto D'onde escono); \"Oibò\" (libretto Ohibò); \"Cos' hai?\" on two notes; \"pentiti\" in lower case at bar 91; \"vè!\" (libretto v'è); \"sò\" with its accent; \"foco\". Bar 74's \"mai\" (\"tacciato mai sarò\") is printed \"mia\" in the score, a misprint, and bar 149's \"ceffo\" is printed \"eeffo\"; both are written as the libretto has them. In bars 82-84 the print sets \"ti-mor\" with a hyphen after mor, running on to \"ver-rò!\"; they are two words, timor and verrò. Lines are repeated as the score repeats them, and Leporello's \"Sì\" of the libretto is not in this score (only the Commendatore sings it). The chorus of spirits from below (bars 131-161: \"Tutto a tue colpe è poco! Vieni! c'è un mal peggior!\") sings in the score and is heard with the piano, but has no role and no lines here. IPA, word meanings and translations were drafted for this app (S60), and the IPA was checked against Castel. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
